WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:05.300
To celebrate the release of Jurassic World,
let's take a look at 12 things you probably

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.160
didn't know about the fourth film in the dinosaur
franchise.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:16.299
Although movies now tend to use visual effects
and CGI rather than animatronics, director

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:21.830
Colin Trevorrow was keen to use an animatronic
dinosaur in Jurassic World as a way of paying

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:27.210
tribute to special effects legends like Stan
Winston who brought to life Jurassic Park's

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.410
T-Rex, Velociraptors and Brachiosaurus.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.750
And, on top of that, Trevorrow thought an
animatronic dinosaur would help the actors

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:38.329
achieve the right intensity in their interaction
with the giant creature.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:45.020
An animatronic Apatosaurus was created over
a period of about three months by John Rosengrant

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:46.859
and his team at Legacy Effects.

00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:52.059
The completed Apatosaurus was operated by
Rosengrant and a team of four puppeteers,

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:55.859
thanks to whom the creature could lift and
turn its head, breathe through its nose and

00:00:55.859 --> 00:00:58.309
mouth, and blink and twitch its eyes.

00:00:58.309 --> 00:01:04.309
In fact, Chris Pratt found the creature so
life-like that it gave him goosebumps!

00:01:04.309 --> 00:01:08.630
In tribute to Richard Attenborough and his
character, John Hammond, who featured in the

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:13.250
first two movies as the creator of Jurassic
Park, a memorial statue stands in front of

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:16.260
Jurassic World's Hammond Creation Lab.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.810
Like in the original movie, in his hand, Hammond
has a cane which features a piece of amber

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:25.820
on top with a mosquito inside, a nod to the
fact that dinosaurs were brought back to life

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.270
through DNA found in amber-trapped mosquitos.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:34.640
Jurassic World's Main Street was built from
scratch in the enormous parking lot of an

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:38.000
abandoned Six Flags amusement park in New
Orleans.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.890
The lot used was around the size of six football
fields.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:46.260
While filming was taking place in Hawaii,
four hundred craftspeople constructed the

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:50.930
Main Street sets, which feature everything
you'd find at a real theme park including

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:55.610
restaurants and stores complete with all kinds
of theme park merchandise.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:02:01.299
In the movie, Jurassic World visitors looking
for something to eat have lots of restaurants

00:02:01.299 --> 00:02:06.451
to choose from including Winston's Steakhouse,
which is a nice nod to special effects legend

00:02:06.451 --> 00:02:11.770
Stan Winston, who won an Oscar for his work
on Jurassic Park.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:16.630
The huge raptor arena, where Chris Pratt's
character Owen works, was built for real using

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.970
steel and cement, rather than fake set walls.

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:24.720
Chris Pratt was blown away by the design team's
work on the arena and said it felt strong

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.300
enough to hold real-life dangerous animals!

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:34.340
Many of the movie's interior sets including
the Visitors' Centre, Dr Wu's genetics lab,

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:39.310
and the Control Room were built and filmed
on six stages at Big Easy Studios, which is

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.400
located at NASA's Michoud Assembly Facility
in New Orleans.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:48.080
Other big movies that have filmed at the Louisiana-based
studios include Dawn Of The Planet Of The

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.620
Apes and Terminator: Genisys.

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.900
The Control Room, from which Bryce Dallas
Howard's character Claire watches over the

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:59.210
park, was made as immersive as possible for
the actors.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:02.950
For example, while they were filming scenes
there, the control room's wall of monitors

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.700
displayed real footage, a lot of which had
been shot during the production.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:10.600
Often a movie would place that footage on
the monitors during post-production rather

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.940
than playing it live during filming.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:18.520
Like the first three movies in the franchise,
Jurassic World filmed in Hawaii.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:23.490
Filming took place for just over 30 days on
the islands of Oahu and Kauai, and began at

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:27.440
Honolulu Zoo, which was turned into Jurassic
World's petting zoo.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.400
The design team built a full-scale paddock
for the movie's new genetically modified dinosaur,

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.880
Indominus rex, at Kualoa Ranch, which was
also used for exterior locations such as Gyrosphere

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.000
Valley.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.980
According to producer Patrick Crowley, Jurassic
World's filmmakers wanted to avoid a repeat

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.920
of the hurricane that destroyed all the sets
during filming of the first movie, Jurassic

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:50.920
Park!

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:54.740
So, on the first day of the shoot in Hawaii,
the movie's crew took part in a spiritual

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.900
blessing ceremony, which they believed would
pay homage to the sacred lands where they

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.450
were filming.

00:04:01.450 --> 00:04:06.230
World-renowned palaeontologist Jack Horner
has been a consultant on all four Jurassic

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:07.230
movies.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:11.560
In fact, Horner's book "Digging Dinosaurs"
was an important part of author Michael Crichton's

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:16.150
research when he was writing Jurassic Park,
and the character of Alan Grant was partly

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:17.180
based on Horner.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:22.530
Horner's current research focuses on dinosaur
evolution and includes reverse-evolving a

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.760
dinosaur from a chicken, as birds are dinosaurs'
closest descendants.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.750
On Jurassic World, Horner worked with the
filmmakers as a technical consultant, particularly

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:37.000
on the movie's new genetically-modified hybrid
dinosaur, Indominus rex, and on designing

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.530
the educational displays in the park's visitor
centre.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:46.800
According to Jack Horner, when the original
movie hit the big screen back in 1993, palaeontologists

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.680
were starting to discover that dinosaurs actually
had feathers.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.380
Nowadays, it's known that many carnivorous
dinosaurs likely did have feathers; however

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.340
this is not reflected in the Jurassic films
because, as Horner says, the dinosaurs 'have

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.000
to be consistent through all of the different
movies.'

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:07.590
Well, there you have it, 12 things you probably
didn't know about Jurassic World!

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:12.030
Now, let me know in the comments below, which
is your favourite dinosaur from the Jurassic

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:13.030
movies?

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.840
And what would you like to see happen in a
Jurassic World sequel?

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.930
If you enjoyed this video, do please share
it, hit the thumbs-up button and subscribe

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:23.940
for more things you didn't know, as well as
